Canonical runtime connector contract

Every external source should be represented internally by a connector object equivalent to:

interface IntelligenceConnector {
  connectorId: string;
  provider: string;
  domain: IntelligenceDomain;

  enabled: boolean;

  authentication: {
    type: "NONE" | "API_KEY" | "OAUTH2" | "BEARER";
    secretRef?: string;
  };

  capabilities: {
    search: boolean;
    events: boolean;
    documents: boolean;
    geospatial: boolean;
    embeddings: boolean;
    streaming: boolean;
  };

  protocol:
    | "REST"
    | "GRAPHQL"
    | "RSS"
    | "ATOM"
    | "JSON"
    | "XML"
    | "CSV"
    | "STIX"
    | "TAXII"
    | "WMS"
    | "WFS";

  endpoint?: string;

  quota: {
    requestsPerMinute?: number;
    requestsPerDay?: number;
    estimatedCostPerRequest?: number;
    freeTier?: boolean;
    runtimeVerified: boolean;
  };

  trust: {
    initialScore: number;
    dynamic: boolean;
  };

  provenance: {
    sourceUrl: boolean;
    retrievalTimestamp: boolean;
    contentHash: boolean;
    sourceTimestamp: boolean;
  };

  normalization: {
    schemaVersion: string;
    deterministic: boolean;
  };
}

Canonical acquisition flow

SOURCE DISCOVERY
      ↓
CAPABILITY DISCOVERY
      ↓
AUTHENTICATION / SECRET RESOLUTION
      ↓
QUOTA DISCOVERY
      ↓
COST POLICY CHECK
      ↓
CONNECTOR HEALTH CHECK
      ↓
REQUEST
      ↓
RATE-LIMIT / RETRY CONTROL
      ↓
RAW RESPONSE CAPTURE
      ↓
CONTENT HASH
      ↓
T0 ATOMIC EVENT
      ↓
VALIDATION
      ↓
DEDUPLICATION
      ↓
ENTITY RESOLUTION
      ↓
ENRICHMENT
      ↓
T1 FEATURE EXTRACTION
      ↓
GRAPH / VECTOR INDEXING
      ↓
RETRIEVAL
      ↓
T2 HYPOTHESIS GENERATION
      ↓
BAYESIAN FUSION
      ↓
UNCERTAINTY / CONTRADICTION ANALYSIS
      ↓
TRACEABLE OUTPUT

Critical architectural distinction

The application should not interpret:

GEMINI_API_KEY exists

as:

Gemini is available

Instead:

GEMINI_API_KEY
      ↓
credential exists?
      ↓
credential valid?
      ↓
project accessible?
      ↓
model available?
      ↓
quota > 0?
      ↓
billing permitted?
      ↓
request permitted by cost policy?
      ↓
HEALTHY / DEGRADED / DISABLED

This matters because Gemini API quota behavior is not equivalent to simply possessing a key; public reports from 2025–2026 document model-specific quota changes and cases where apparently valid keys received zero-quota responses.

Therefore the connector manager should expose:

type ProviderState =
  | "AVAILABLE"
  | "DEGRADED"
  | "QUOTA_EXHAUSTED"
  | "AUTH_FAILURE"
  | "BILLING_REQUIRED"
  | "MODEL_UNAVAILABLE"
  | "POLICY_BLOCKED"
  | "DISABLED";

and select providers dynamically rather than failing the entire intelligence pipeline.

Recommended free/public-source strategy

The highest-value architectural change is to distinguish keyless public sources from credentialed providers.

For example:

KEYLESS PUBLIC SOURCES
├── Government open data
├── Public advisories
├── Public RSS / Atom
├── GDELT
├── Public documents
├── Public geographic datasets
├── Public registries
├── Public vulnerability catalogs
├── Public STIX/TAXII feeds
└── Open research datasets

OPTIONAL API-KEY SOURCES
├── Gemini
├── Commercial geocoding
├── Commercial threat intelligence
├── Commercial financial data
├── Commercial social APIs
└── Other operator-authorized providers

This prevents an unavailable API key from becoming a single point of failure.

For cybersecurity, for example, CISA's KEV catalog is explicitly a maintained public catalog of known exploited vulnerabilities, making it an appropriate public-data connector rather than something that needs an invented API credential.

Required secret file

Keep the real secret-bearing configuration separate from the public connector configuration:

# .env.local
# NEVER COMMIT THIS FILE

GEMINI_API_KEY=<runtime-secret>

APP_URL=<deployment-url>

POSTGRES_URL=<runtime-secret>
NEO4J_URI=<runtime-secret>
NEO4J_USERNAME=<runtime-secret>
NEO4J_PASSWORD=<runtime-secret>

REDIS_URL=<runtime-secret>

KAFKA_BROKERS=<runtime-secret>

MILVUS_URI=<runtime-secret>

OBJECT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=<runtime-secret>
OBJECT_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY=<runtime-secret>
OBJECT_STORAGE_SECRET_KEY=<runtime-secret>

And commit only a sanitized template:

# .env.example

GEMINI_API_KEY=

APP_URL=

POSTGRES_URL=
NEO4J_URI=
NEO4J_USERNAME=
NEO4J_PASSWORD=

REDIS_URL=
KAFKA_BROKERS=

MILVUS_URI=

OBJECT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=
OBJECT_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY=
OBJECT_STORAGE_SECRET_KEY=

The application should never contain:

const GEMINI_API_KEY = "AIza...";

or:

NEXT_PUBLIC_GEMINI_API_KEY = "...";

because either pattern can expose the credential to source control or the browser.

Canonical provenance record

Every acquired artifact should ultimately produce something equivalent to:

{
  "trace_id": "trace-...",
  "event_id": "evt-...",
  "epistemic_level": "T0",

  "source": {
    "connector_id": "connector-...",
    "provider": "...",
    "source_url": "...",
    "source_timestamp": "...",
    "retrieval_timestamp": "..."
  },

  "integrity": {
    "content_hash": "sha256:...",
    "schema_version": "4.1",
    "connector_version": "..."
  },

  "observation": {
    "entity_ids": [],
    "entity_type": "...",
    "signal_type": "...",
    "payload": {}
  },

  "quality": {
    "source_trust": 0.0,
    "observation_confidence": 0.0,
    "completeness": 0.0,
    "timeliness": 0.0,
    "validity": 0.0
  }
}

Then T1 can reference the T0 artifact:

{
  "feature_id": "feat-...",
  "epistemic_level": "T1",
  "parent_event_ids": [
    "evt-..."
  ],
  "feature_type": "...",
  "value": 0.0,
  "algorithm": "...",
  "algorithm_version": "...",
  "computed_at": "...",
  "deterministic": true
}

And T2 can reference both:

{
  "hypothesis_id": "hyp-...",
  "epistemic_level": "T2",

  "parent_events": [
    "evt-..."
  ],

  "parent_features": [
    "feat-..."
  ],

  "hypothesis": "...",

  "prior_probability": 0.0,
  "posterior_probability": 0.0,

  "confidence": 0.0,
  "entropy": 0.0,

  "model": {
    "provider": "google",
    "model": "...",
    "version": "..."
  },

  "evidence_trace": [
    "evt-...",
    "feat-..."
  ]
}

This preserves the central UIF-OS invariant:

T0 → T1 → T2

while preventing:

T2 → overwrite T0
T2 → overwrite T1

Provider-selection algorithm

The runtime should score connectors rather than hard-code one provider:

ProviderScore =
    availability
  × quota
  × provenance_quality
  × data_quality
  × capability_match
  × policy_compliance
  × cost_score
  × latency_score

A provider is rejected if:

authentication invalid
OR
quota exhausted
OR
policy disallows provider
OR
cost exceeds configured budget
OR
source is unavailable
OR
schema validation fails

This gives you a true multi-provider scientific acquisition fabric instead of a collection of environment variables.

Final architecture

                    ┌──────────────────────────────┐
                    │       PUBLIC DATA SOURCES     │
                    ├──────────────────────────────┤
                    │ OSINT                         │
                    │ SOCMINT                       │
                    │ GEOINT                        │
                    │ CTI                           │
                    │ GOVSEC                        │
                    │ GDELT                         │
                    │ NETWORK                       │
                    │ ECONOMIC                      │
                    │ MARITIME                      │
                    │ AVIATION                      │
                    │ SCIENCE                       │
                    │ HUMANITARIAN                  │
                    │ GOVERNMENT                    │
                    │ MEDIA                         │
                    └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                                   │
                                   ▼
                    ┌──────────────────────────────┐
                    │ CONNECTOR ORCHESTRATOR        │
                    │                              │
                    │ discovery                    │
                    │ capability detection         │
                    │ authentication               │
                    │ quota detection              │
                    │ cost governance              │
                    │ retry/backoff                 │
                    │ circuit breakers              │
                    │ caching                       │
                    └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                                   │
                                   ▼
                    ┌──────────────────────────────┐
                    │      T0 EVENT FABRIC          │
